  "body": "Part of #2525 (slice 1 of 6). The maintainer set the priority on 2026-11-18: \"GPU shader issues get priority\".\n\n## What to add\n\nAdd a CPU module in `camcraft-geometry` (for example `crates/geometry/src/nesting/raster/`) that converts a `geo2d::Region` to a bit-packed grid of cells. This is the base of the raster nest of #2525. It has no GPU code and no wgpu.\n\n- A grid has an origin, a cell size (integer, in `geo2d::SCALE` units) and a width and height in cells. Rows are packed in `u32` words.\n- **Over-approximate mode** (for parts): mark each cell that the region touches. A cell that only touches the boundary is marked.\n- **Under-approximate mode** (for the usable sheet): mark a cell free only when the full cell is inside the region. Holes of the region are not free.\n- Use integer arithmetic on the `geo2d` integer coordinates only. No float enters the decision of a cell.\n- Refuse by name: a non-positive cell size, a grid larger than a stated cell limit, a non-finite input, an empty region where one is required.\n- One-line provenance comment: scanline polygon fill and conservative coverage, from Bennell and Oliveira (EJOR 2008) and general computer-graphics literature.\n\n## Why\n\nThe raster finds nest positions fast. It is only a first filter. A raster \"free\" result must be a true \"free\" result: parts over-approximate, the sheet under-approximates (#2525, research comment 2).\n\n## Notes\n\n- No new crate edge, no new dependency, no decision entry for an edge. Record the cell-size default and the cell limit in `docs/decisions/<issue>-<slug>.md`.\n- No MCP tool: this is a kernel module with no command.\n- Work that changes nest geometry is under the safety floor: `effort:high`.\n- Headless tests only. No GPU.\n\n## Done means\n\nTests in `crates/geometry/tests/` show:\n- For squares, a part with a hole, a thin sliver and a rotated part, each point of the region lies in a marked cell (over-approximate), checked on a dense point sample.\n- Each free cell of the under-approximate sheet lies fully inside the region (all four corners and the cell centre inside, and no region edge crosses the cell).\n- The same input gives the same bits on each run.\n- Each refusal names its reason.\n",
